faq:
  - q: What is the difference between social anxiety and shyness?
    a: Shyness is a personal style; it can bring tension but does not narrow life. In social phobia (social anxiety disorder) the fear is out of proportion to the situation, spills over into the time before and after it, leads to avoidance and affects work, school or relationships.
  - q: Are social anxiety and panic attacks the same thing?
    a: No. The World Health Organization (WHO) describes them as separate anxiety disorders. In social anxiety the fear is about being humiliated, embarrassed or rejected. In panic disorder, the attacks and the fear of having another one are at the centre. Intense anxiety in a social setting can also come with panic-like bodily symptoms; talking it through with a professional makes the distinction clearer.
  - q: Could a shy child have social anxiety?
    a: Not every shy child has social anxiety. NICE's social anxiety guideline asks health and education professionals to be alert to possible anxiety disorders in children who avoid school or group activities, find it hard to talk in social situations, or seem excessively shy.
  - q: Is online therapy suitable for social anxiety?
    a: If meeting face to face feels hard at first, an online session can make the first step easier. Which format suits you is discussed together in the first meeting. At our centre, sessions can be held both in person and online.
  - q: When should I think about getting support?
    a: If you are giving up things you want because of anxiety, if social situations occupy your mind for a long time before and after, or if they are affecting your work, studies or relationships, you can talk this through with a psychologist.
