blocks:
  - type: metin
    baslik: What is social anxiety?
    metin: |-
      Social phobia is today more often called **social anxiety disorder** in clinical language. The World Health Organization (WHO) describes it as high levels of fear and worry about social situations that might make a person feel humiliated, embarrassed or rejected ([WHO, 2026](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/anxiety-disorders)).

      The National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) adds a further detail: this fear is persistent and out of proportion to the actual threat the situation poses. The person fears doing or saying something they would find humiliating or embarrassing. Blushing, sweating, shaking, appearing boring or incompetent, and looking anxious are among the most common of these fears ([NICE, 2013](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg159/chapter/Context)).
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: In which situations does social anxiety appear?
    giris: 'NICE lists typical situations that can provoke the anxiety ([NICE, 2013](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg159/chapter/Context)):'
    maddeler:
      - baslik: Meeting new people, starting conversations
      - baslik: Speaking in meetings or in groups
      - baslik: Talking to authority figures
      - baslik: Eating, drinking or working while being watched
      - baslik: Going shopping, being seen in public
      - baslik: Public speaking or performing
  - type: metin
    baslik: What is shyness?
    metin: |-
      Shyness is not a diagnosis. It is an everyday word for being reserved in new settings, needing time to warm up, or preferring small groups to crowds. A shy person may tense up among people they do not know, but they settle over time and the tension does not get in the way of what they want to do.

      NICE also notes that worries about some of these situations are common in the general population. What makes the difference is how strong the worry is and how long it lasts ([NICE, 2013](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg159/chapter/Context)).
  - type: kart-grid
    baslik: The difference between social anxiety and shyness
    giris: 'Set side by side, the two differ in four ways:'
    kolon: 2
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Shyness
        maddeler:
          - 'Intensity: tension, but manageable'
          - 'Timing: within the situation, short-lived'
          - 'Avoidance: joins in, even with effort'
          - 'Effect: does not narrow life'
      - baslik: Social anxiety
        maddeler:
          - 'Intensity: fear out of proportion'
          - 'Timing: spills into before and after'
          - 'Avoidance: starts staying away'
          - 'Effect: affects work, school, relationships'
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: The four differences up close
    kartlar:
      - baslik: '1. Intensity: is the fear in proportion?'
        metin: A shy person gets nervous before a presentation. Someone with social anxiety feels almost certain they will be humiliated in the same presentation. A clear gap opens between the real risk of the situation and the fear that is felt.
      - baslik: '2. Timing: does the anxiety outlast the situation?'
        metin: According to NICE, people with social anxiety disorder worry excessively about these situations not only at the time but also before and afterwards ([NICE, 2013](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg159/chapter/Context)). Worrying about an invitation days in advance, or going over what you said again and again after a conversation has ended, are familiar parts of this cycle.
      - baslik: '3. Avoidance: have you started saying no?'
        metin: A shy person joins in, even if it is hard. With social anxiety, staying away from feared situations can slowly become a habit. Texting instead of calling, not asking questions in class, or skipping a meeting with "something came up" are examples. Avoidance brings relief in the moment, but it also stops the feared situation from ever being tested.
      - baslik: '4. Effect: is your life getting smaller?'
        metin: "NICE notes that social anxiety disorder can disrupt daily life, affect relationships and quality of life, and impair performance at work or school. It adds that some people use alcohol or drugs to try to reduce their anxiety ([NICE, 2013](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg159/chapter/Context)). The deciding question is this: are you giving up things you want because of anxiety?"
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: Two questions you can ask yourself
    giris: 'NICE suggests two simple questions to help health professionals notice social anxiety ([NICE, 2013](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg159/chapter/1-Recommendations)):'
    maddeler:
      - baslik: Do you find yourself avoiding social situations or activities?
      - baslik: Are you fearful or embarrassed in social situations?
    kapanis: These questions do not make a diagnosis. But if you answer "yes" to both, talking it through with a professional can help you understand what you are going through.
  - type: metin
    baslik: How does social anxiety show in children and teenagers?
    metin: |-
      According to NICE, children may show anxiety differently from adults. As well as shrinking from interactions, they may cry, freeze or have tantrums. Taking part in classroom activities, asking for help in class, joining activities with peers and school performances are situations that can be hard at these ages ([NICE, 2013](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg159/chapter/Context)).

      Not every shy child has social anxiety. But if reserve is making it hard to go to school, our article on [school refusal](/en/blog/school-refusal-in-children.html) may help. We work with high-school-age young people within [adolescent psychology](/en/services/adolescent-psychology.html).
  - type: metin
    baslik: Not a character flaw
    metin: |-
      NICE notes that people with social anxiety may not know it is a recognised condition and may see their anxiety as a personal flaw. The same people may also find it hard to make contact with health professionals ([NICE, 2013](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg159/chapter/1-Recommendations)). This is why the first step can be even harder for someone living with this difficulty.

      If meeting face to face feels hard at first, the first session can be held through [online therapy](/en/services/online-therapy.html).
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: What is worked on in therapy?
    giris: 'Our centre does not hold to a single theory. The approach is decided together with the individual:'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT)
        metin: The work focuses on expectations about social situations, thoughts such as "everyone is looking at me", and the pattern of avoidance. [CBT page](/en/services/cbt-therapy.html)
      - baslik: Acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT)
        metin: Rather than struggling to get rid of the anxiety, the aim is to take steps towards what matters to you even while the anxiety is there. [ACT page](/en/services/act-therapy.html)
      - baslik: Psychodynamic therapy
        metin: The work tries to understand the roots of shame and recurring relationship patterns. [Psychodynamic therapy page](/en/services/psychodynamic-therapy.html)
  - type: metin
    metin: |-
      Sessions last 50 minutes and are scheduled weekly or every two weeks, in Turkish or English. If you are wondering when getting support makes sense, you can read our article on [when to go to therapy](/en/blog/when-to-go-to-therapy.html), and [write to us](/en/contact.html) with any questions.
